bnn-lowest.original_statement_md:
Theorem 2.2. Let $\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}$ denote the lowest degree of the expansion terms of the interaction effect $I(S\mid\mathbf x')$, i.e., $\forall i\in S,\widehat\pi_i=1;\forall i\notin S,\widehat\pi_i=0$. Let us consider the interaction effect $I(S\mid\mathbf x')$ only containing the expansion term of the lowest degree, i.e., $I(S\mid\mathbf x')=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x')$. In this way, the mean and variance of the interaction effect $I(S\mid\mathbf x'=\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)$ over different perturbations $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ are given as

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}},\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}^2((1+(\sigma/\tau)^2)^{|S|}-1).\quad\text{(27)}
\end{aligned}$$

bnn-lowest.original_proof_md:
Proof. If we only consider Taylor expansion term of the lowest degree, then $I(S\mid\mathbf x')=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x')$, where $J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x')=\prod_{i\in S}\operatorname{sign}(x'_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x'_i-r_i}\tau$.

Let us add a Gaussian perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ to the input sample $\mathbf x$. In this way, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&\approx U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)\\
J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i}\tau\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-r_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right).\quad\text{(28)}
\end{aligned}$$

According to the setting of the reference value in Section 2.3, we have $\forall i\in S,x_i-r_i\in\{-\tau,\tau\}$. Also in Section 2.3, we have assumed that the variance of the perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ is small, so that we can ignore the extremely low probability that the perturbation is large such that $|\epsilon_i|\ge\tau$. In this way, we have $\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)=\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)$, and we can obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-r_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right).\quad\text{(29)}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\right].\quad\text{(30)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\in\{-1,1\}$, we have $1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\sim\mathcal N(1,(\sigma/\tau)^2)$, $\forall i\in S$.

Proposition G.1. If random variables $X_1,X_2,\cdots,X_k$ are independent of each other, then $\mathbb E[X_1X_2\cdots X_k]=\prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb E[X_i]$, and $\operatorname{Var}[X_1X_2\cdots X_k]=\prod_{i=1}^k(\mathbb E[X_i]^2+\operatorname{Var}[X_i])-\prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb E[X_i]^2$.

According to the above proposition, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}1=1\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}(1^2+(\sigma/\tau)^2)-\prod_{i\in S}1^2\\
&=(1+(\sigma/\tau)^2)^{|S|}-1.\quad\text{(31)}
\end{aligned}$$

Therefore,

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon[U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}^2\left((1+(\sigma/\tau)^2)^{|S|}-1\right).\quad\text{(32)}
\end{aligned}$$

bnn-general-moments.original_statement_md:
Theorem 2.3. Let $\boldsymbol\pi\in Q_S=\{[\pi_1,\ldots,\pi_n]\mid\forall i\in S,\pi_i\in\mathbb N^+;\forall i\notin S,\pi_i=0\}$ denote an arbitrary degree. Then, the mean and the variance of $J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)$ over perturbations $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ are

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right],\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right].\quad\text{(33)}
\end{aligned}$$

bnn-general-moments.original_proof_md:
Proof. According to Lemma 2.1, given an arbitrary input sample $\mathbf x'$, we have

$$J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x')=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x'_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x'_i-r_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\qquad\text{(34)}$$

Let us add a Gaussian perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ to the input sample $\mathbf x$. In this way, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-r_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\quad\text{(35)}
\end{aligned}$$

According to the setting of the reference value in Section 2.3, $\forall i\in S,x_i-r_i\in\{-\tau,\tau\}$. Also, in Section 2.3, we have assumed that the variance of the perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ is small, so that we can ignore the extremely low probability that the perturbation is large such that $|\epsilon_i|\ge\tau$. In this way, $\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-r_i)=\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)$, and we can obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-r_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\quad\text{(36)}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right].\quad\text{(37)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\forall i\in S,\epsilon_i$ is independent of each other, according to Proposition G.1 and Eq. (37), we have

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{2\pi_i}\right]-\prod_{i\in S}\left(\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\right)^2.\quad\text{(38)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\in\{-1,1\}$, we have $\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}[(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-r_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau)^k]=\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}[(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau)^k]$, $\forall k\in\mathbb N^+$. Therefore, we obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{2\pi_i}\right]-\prod_{i\in S}\left(\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\right)^2\\
&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right].
\end{aligned}$$

bnn-growth.original_statement_md:
Theorem 2.4. Let $S$ and $S'$ be two interactive concepts, such that $S\subsetneq S'$. Let us consider expansion terms $J(S,\boldsymbol\pi)$ and $J(S',\boldsymbol\pi')$, where the term $J(S',\boldsymbol\pi')$ is extended from the term $J(S,\boldsymbol\pi)$ with $\boldsymbol\pi\prec\boldsymbol\pi'$. I.e., (1) $\forall i\in S',\pi'_i\in\mathbb N^+$; otherwise, $\pi'_i=0$. (2) Given $\boldsymbol\pi'$, $\forall j\in S,\pi_j=\pi'_j$; otherwise, $\pi_j=0$. Then, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S',\boldsymbol\pi'\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]}{\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]}&>\prod_{i\in S'\setminus S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}^{,2}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi'_i}\right],\\
\frac{\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S',\boldsymbol\pi'\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]/\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S',\boldsymbol\pi'\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]}{\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]/\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]}&<\frac{1}{\prod_{i\in S'\setminus S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi'_i}\right]}.\quad\text{(39)}
\end{aligned}$$

and we can also obtain $\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}[(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau)^{\pi'_i}]\ge1$.
